armada — agents that work while you do not

USAGE
  armada <module> <verb> [flags]  the verbs Armada owns
  armada helm                     enter Helm, the agent you talk to

MANIFEST — what a workspace is and how to operate it
  config <scan|verify>  propose what the repository proves, then verify it
  init                  claim this workspace: ports, .armada/, setup
  up [<component>]      start this workspace's services; wait until they answer
  down [<component>]    stop this workspace's services; keep the port block
  status                what is running, what is mine, what is stale
  check [<selector>]    lint, format and test — the verb a gate calls
  skills [show <name>]  what this repository knows about itself
  components            what this repository can be filtered by
  commands              the verbs this repository declares
  clean                 release what this workspace owns
  prune                 reclaim docker disk, including disk that is not armada's
  <name>                a verb this repo declares; manifest commands lists them

GUILD — you, portable: voice, skills, hooks, workflows
  init             build your guild by interviewing you
  project          put your guild where Claude Code will read it
  pull             bring this machine's guild up to date
  upgrade          take what Armada has learned since your guild was made
  push             send local guild changes to the remote
  export           write the whole guild to one portable file
  import <bundle>  restore a guild from a bundle
  ls               see what is in your guild
  show <item>      print one guild file
  edit <item>      open one guild file, validate it, and commit it
  delete <item>    remove one thing from your guild, and commit the removal

FLEET — the agents you do not talk to: Jobs, worktrees, budgets
  spawn <task>          start an isolated agent Job on a task
  ls                    what is running, what it has spent, and who needs you
  show <job>            one Job in full, and why it needs you
  board <job>           take a Job over yourself: worktree and resume command
  answer <id> <answer>  give a waiting Job your decision; let it continue
  inbox                 what the fleet needs from you
  pause <job>           stop a Job's Drone and keep everything else
  resume <job>          start a paused Job's session again
  tick [<job>]          gate the step it rests on, then advance, retry or stop
  reap                  end every finished Job and release what they hold
  kill [<job>]          end a Job and release everything it owns

HELM AND THE BRIDGE — where you talk, and what you watch
  helm          the one agent you talk to: wire the toolbelt, report the launch
  helm enable   let --exec become a session, on this machine
  helm disable  the opposite of enable — the default on a fresh install
  bridge        the live screen: every Job, its state, its spend, who needs you

THIS MACHINE
  init      set up this machine: checks, ~/.armada/, and your guild
  doctor    what this machine is missing, and what fixes it
  settings  every setting: its value, where it lives, whether it syncs
  failures  what Armada broke on, and how to put a Job on one
  report    tell Armada something went wrong that it did not notice
  task      write down a thing to do, and spend nothing on it
  tasks     what you wrote down, and how to put a Job on one
  untried   which of Armada's verbs you have never run here

GLOBAL FLAGS
  --json          answer as one JSON object, for a script or an agent
  --color <when>  auto (default), always, never; NO_COLOR wins
  -h, --help      this page, or a verb's own flags
  -V, --version   the version

NOT BUILT YET
  armada guild verify                         M2
  armada manifest render, agents-md, explain  reserved

  Each is claimed and answers `not built yet`, so that a name which is
  going to mean one thing never means something else first.

Run `armada <module> <verb> --help` for a verb's own flags.

Global flags come before the module. Everything after a commands: name is the
child's, including flags Armada itself defines.
